WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.190
<v Instructor>Okay, so now let's talk about RDS.</v>

2
00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:05.010
So RDS is going to be small at the exam.

3
00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.900
We just need to know how many engines there are.

4
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.610
So by now, hopefully, you should know them.

5
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.640
So there's PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB,

6
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.870
IBM DB2, Oracle, and Microsoft SQL Server.

7
00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:16.890
It's a managed database.

8
00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.840
You need to provision the servers though.

9
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.430
You choose like the instance type,

10
00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.770
and then you get a bunch of things out of it:

11
00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.250
automated backups, patching, monitoring, and so on.

12
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.860
You usually launch your RDS database in a VPC,

13
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.210
in a private subnet,

14
00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.280
and then you control access to your database

15
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:33.840
using security groups.

16
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.880
And remember, when we have a Lambda function

17
00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.160
who wants to access our RDS database,

18
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.430
we have to launch it into a private VPC subnet

19
00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.150
so that it has communication with our RDS database.

20
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.280
The storage is on EBS volumes,

21
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.860
and you can increase the volume size automatically

22
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.810
thanks to auto-scaling.

23
00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:52.643
For backups,

24
00:00:52.643 --> 00:00:54.330
you get automated with point-in-time recoveries,

25
00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.010
and the backups will expire,

26
00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.510
and the snapshots are very similar,

27
00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:58.590
but they're manual,

28
00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.600
in which case you can take a backup,

29
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.740
and have it in point-of-time

30
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.350
for how many years you want.

31
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.450
And it's possible for you to take these snapshots,

32
00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.220
and copy them across regions,

33
00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:09.060
which is very helpful

34
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.820
when you want to have disaster recovery.

35
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.900
RDS also publishes a bunch of events

36
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.160
about what's happening to your database.

37
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.480
So you can get an SNS topic

38
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.090
that will contain all the events for your RDS.

39
00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.950
It's called RDS event notifications.

40
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.650
And this could be for operations, for outages,

41
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.950
for when the backups are beginning, et cetera, et cetera.

42
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.020
So here's another concept that you should know by now.

43
00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.310
There's Multi-AZ,

44
00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.800
in which you have a standby instance for failover

45
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.330
in case there's an outage.

46
00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.990
So we have a Master RDS Database, in one availability zone,

47
00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.900
and it's going to be synchronously replicated

48
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.580
to another standby instance,

49
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.950
in another AZ, so, AZ B.

50
00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.340
Your application only accesses your RDS database

51
00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.230
through one DNS name,

52
00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.870
and that DNS name will change automatically

53
00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.430
in case of a failover.

54
00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.470
And so that means that your standby database is

55
00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.060
never accessed by your application,

56
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.340
only your master database is,

57
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.520
and there will be reads and writes happening directly,

58
00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.100
solely onto the master instance.

59
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.910
So here, multi AZ is really for recovery in case of outage.

60
00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:12.743
It's a backup database.

61
00:02:12.743 --> 00:02:14.010
It's a standby database,

62
00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.830
but our application never uses it.

63
00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:18.720
And if we wanted to scale the reads,

64
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.790
then we would use read replicas.

65
00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.380
So these read replicas are going

66
00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.540
to be asynchronous replication,

67
00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.700
so we have eventual consistency,

68
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.710
and they can be created cross-region.

69
00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.330
So it's possible to use replica

70
00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:34.380
to reduce the latencies in different regions in AWS.

71
00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.130
So your RDS instance will have an asynchronous replication

72
00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:39.960
into another RDS read replica,

73
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.350
and then, again, same region or different region.

74
00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.260
And then your application can do reads and writes

75
00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.720
into your main RDS database,

76
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.350
but can also do reads-only onto your read replica.

77
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.990
This is why it's called a read replica.

78
00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.610
So once you have read replicas,

79
00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.170
you may want to actually distribute

80
00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.240
the reads of your application.

81
00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.640
And to remove some application logic,

82
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.340
we're going to use Route 53.

83
00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.720
So using Route 53,

84
00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:07.680
what's going to happen is

85
00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.700
that you can create a weighted record set, okay?

86
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:12.630
And, in this example,

87
00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:16.950
I set the weight of every read replica endpoint to 25%,

88
00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.570
but we can set it to whatever we want.

89
00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.570
But if all your read replicas are having

90
00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.130
the same specifications,

91
00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:25.380
then it's a good idea to set the same weight.

92
00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.010
And we can also enable health checks within Amazon Route 53,

93
00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.890
so that if an RDS replica is having issues,

94
00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:35.010
we don't include it in our record.

95
00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:35.843
And, therefore,

96
00:03:35.843 --> 00:03:38.250
whenever an application is going to make a request

97
00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.450
to Route 53,

98
00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.240
and imagine we have a thousand applications doing this,

99
00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.713
then each of them is going to be spread 25% on each replica.

100
00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:49.830
In terms of security,

101
00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.090
KMS encryption is available

102
00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.580
for your underlying EBS volume and snapshots.

103
00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.160
You get transparent data inscription, TDE,

104
00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.140
for Oracle and SQL Server.

105
00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.420
You get SSL encryption to RDS

106
00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.640
for your database in-flight calls,

107
00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.500
and it's possible to enforce it.

108
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.460
IAM authentication works for MySQL, PostgreSQL, and MariaDB.

109
00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:09.540
And authorization,

110
00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:11.370
even though you're authenticated with IAM,

111
00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.050
still happens within RDS.

112
00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:14.070
So you still need to define

113
00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.720
what your users can and cannot do

114
00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.610
inside of your RDS database.

115
00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:19.740
IAM cannot help you with that.

116
00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:24.740
You can create an encrypted RDS snapshot

117
00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.480
from an unencrypted RDS snapshot.

118
00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.910
So that means that you can encrypt an unencrypted database

119
00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.950
through the process of snapshotting.

120
00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.200
And CloudTrail, as a general reminder,

121
00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.503
cannot be used to track queries made within RDS.

122
00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:42.060
So if we do a deeper dive into RDS IAM authentication,

123
00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.330
it's available for MariaDB, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.

124
00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.490
And, in case you use IAM authentication,

125
00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:48.930
you do not need a passport.

126
00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.520
You just get an authentication token

127
00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.850
that you obtain by using the RDS API calls.

128
00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:55.830
You should know that this token has a lifetime

129
00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:57.120
of 15 minutes,

130
00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.310
but it's only used to connect through database,

131
00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:00.480
then you're good to go.

132
00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:01.560
If you want to reconnect,

133
00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.620
you would need to get a new authentication token.

134
00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.453
So does that work?

135
00:05:05.453 --> 00:05:07.530
Well, for example, let's say we have an EC2 instance

136
00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.060
with the proper IAM role,

137
00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.120
that allows us to get an off token from the RDS service

138
00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.700
by doing an API call to AWS.

139
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:19.140
Then, using this off token on an SSL encrypted connection,

140
00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.700
we can connect to the database,

141
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.360
and, of course, security groups must be properly configured.

142
00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:25.200
So the benefits of this,

143
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.440
that, A, you only get SSL encrypted traffic,

144
00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.230
then you have IAM to centrally manage the users

145
00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.750
instead of managing them from within the database.

146
00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:35.130
And you can leverage IAM roles,

147
00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:36.900
and EC2 instance profiles,

148
00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.810
for a very easy integration.

149
00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.650
So next we have the RDS specificity for Oracle.

150
00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:45.150
And I wish you didn't have to learn them

151
00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.170
because it's quite boring,

152
00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:47.370
but you have to,

153
00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:48.870
and they can come up at the exam

154
00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.340
for one or two questions.

155
00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:51.990
So let's talk about backups.

156
00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.480
So you have two ways of backing up an Oracle database.

157
00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:55.800
You have RDS Backups,

158
00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.710
and then it's something else called Oracle RMAN.

159
00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:02.730
So RDS Backups are used to back up, and restore,

160
00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:05.700
to Amazon RDS for Oracle,

161
00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.130
whereas Oracle RMAN, Recovery Manager,

162
00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.090
are to backup up and restore to non-RDS.

163
00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:13.740
Okay, RDS is not supported.

164
00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.180
So a graph will help.

165
00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:17.490
RDS backups obviously must be backups

166
00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.010
of your RDS Database Instances,

167
00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:21.690
and then they can only be restored

168
00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.360
into an RDS Database Instance.

169
00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.680
Whereas, again, RMAN works for RDS Database Instance,

170
00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.050
in terms of, yes, you can back it up,

171
00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.810
but then the restore only happens

172
00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.030
for an external Oracle database.

173
00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:39.150
It does not happen for an RDS database instance.

174
00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.640
So the flow is database instance,

175
00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.350
RDS Database Instance of Oracle,

176
00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.500
to RDS Backup, to RDS Database Instance of Oracle,

177
00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:50.850
or RDS Database Instance of Oracle, to RMAN Backup,

178
00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:54.810
to Amazon S3, to an external Oracle database

179
00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:57.720
that is living outside of RDS.

180
00:06:57.720 --> 00:06:59.850
Now, there's nothing called Real Applications Cluster.

181
00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:00.930
So RAC.

182
00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:03.180
RDS for Oracle does not support RAC.

183
00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:04.740
It could be an exam trick.

184
00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:08.940
RAC can only be working on Oracle on EC2 instances

185
00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.490
because you have full control of Oracle

186
00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.220
when you set it up on an EC2 instance.

187
00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:16.020
Oracle does support TDE,

188
00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:18.510
to encrypt data before it's written into storage.

189
00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:20.730
And DMS works on Oracle RDS.

190
00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:23.910
So you can do an Oracle database on-premises

191
00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.590
that you wanna replicate into the Cloud.

192
00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:27.420
For this, you just use DMS

193
00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.070
to replicate and migrate the data,

194
00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.743
from the Oracle Database on-premises to the Cloud.

195
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.910
And there are some other exam tips you need to know for RDS

196
00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:37.080
for MySQL.

197
00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.870
So you can use the native MySQL dump tool

198
00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:41.970
to migrate the MySQL RDS database

199
00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:44.910
into a non-RDS MySQL database.

200
00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:45.743
For this,

201
00:07:45.743 --> 00:07:49.080
this external non-RDS database can be either on-premises,

202
00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:49.913
in your data center,

203
00:07:49.913 --> 00:07:50.940
or you can run it on your own,

204
00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:54.480
for example, on an Amazon EC2 Instance.

205
00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:58.440
So you have the RDS MySQL Database Instance in your Cloud,

206
00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.110
and then the target may be, for example, on-premises.

207
00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:03.600
And, simply, you will use, as a DB admin,

208
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:06.060
the MySQL dump tool to export it,

209
00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.520
import the data back into where you want it to go.

210
00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.260
Then you start a replication mechanism

211
00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:12.510
between the two databases to synchronize the data.

212
00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.850
And then, once the replication is done,

213
00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:17.553
you stop it, and you stop the source database.

214
00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:21.540
Another feature for RDS is to use the RDS Proxy,

215
00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:23.460
for example, with Lambda functions.

216
00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.800
So when you use Lambda functions with the RDS,

217
00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.470
they open, and maintain, a database connection.

218
00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.230
But sometimes having too many Lambdas can result

219
00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:33.750
in a "TooManyConnections" exception.

220
00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.850
So thanks to the RDS Proxy,

221
00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:37.230
you no longer need code

222
00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:39.510
that will handle cleaning up idle connections

223
00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.160
and managing connection pool.

224
00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:43.770
You just connect to the proxy and you're good to go.

225
00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:46.620
So this proxy supports IAM authentication,

226
00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:47.940
or database authentication,

227
00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:49.500
as well as auto-scaling.

228
00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.750
So the idea is that you deploy a proxy,

229
00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:54.750
and this proxy has to be deployed in a private subnet,

230
00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:57.480
because it cannot be publicly accessible.

231
00:08:57.480 --> 00:08:59.070
And this proxy is linked, for example,

232
00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:02.040
to your Aurora database cluster.

233
00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.870
Now your Lambda functions are also going

234
00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:06.030
to be deployed within your VPC

235
00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.850
to get connectivity to your RDS Proxy.

236
00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.200
And now as a result,

237
00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:13.920
all the connections to your number of database are pooled

238
00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.810
through the RDS Proxy.

239
00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:16.643
And that's it,

240
00:09:16.643 --> 00:09:19.380
that solves the problem of too many connections exception.

241
00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:22.080
Finally, one solution architecture we've already seen,

242
00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.020
which is how to do a cross-region failure with RDS,

243
00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:27.120
would be to check the database health,

244
00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.760
either using a instance, for example,

245
00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.280
that has a slash health DB route to check,

246
00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:33.840
or a CloudWatch Alarm.

247
00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:37.950
These things would be checked by Route 53 Health Checks,

248
00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:40.050
that would be linked to a CloudWatch Alarm,

249
00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:42.090
and that would trigger a CloudWatch Event.

250
00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:44.910
And this CloudWatch Event would trigger a Lambda function,

251
00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.060
whose role would be to update a Route 53 DNS,

252
00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:48.893
for the database,

253
00:09:48.893 --> 00:09:51.390
and also promote a read replica

254
00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:52.890
that is maybe in another region.

255
00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.120
For example, us-west-2.

256
00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:56.340
We have a cross-region replication in this instance,

257
00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.500
and then we have effectively created

258
00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:00.780
a cross-region failover architecture.

259
00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:02.160
So this is something very common

260
00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:02.993
that you should know for RDS,

261
00:10:02.993 --> 00:10:04.860
but we already seen this in details.

262
00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:05.693
And that's it.

263
00:10:05.693 --> 00:10:06.930
RDS really is a high level launch

264
00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:08.220
you need to know for the exam,

265
00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:10.050
but you should know enough by now.

